#show: main.with(
  title: "You Do Not Need to Know the Future to Have One",
  desc: "What I would tell someone choosing a life when no one can tell them what the world will become.",
  date: "2026-06-12",
  tags: ("career-and-learning", "ai"),
)

I do not know what I would tell an eighteen-year-old to become today. Computer scientist? Engineer? Designer? Researcher? Something that does not have a name yet? I can tell them what is growing now, what employers seem to want now, and which skills seem useful now. I cannot honestly tell them what the world will reward when they are thirty.

That is an uncomfortable thing for an adult to admit. Young people are asked to make choices that sound permanent at exactly the age when the future is hardest to see. Pick a major. Build a career. Develop expertise. Find the thing you are good at. Now add AI to that uncertainty. A student can reasonably ask whether the skill they spend four years learning will still be scarce by the time they graduate.

I hear versions of this question more often now: Is computer science still worth studying if AI can write code? Should I learn to draw if models can make images? Is graduate school worth it if research itself is changing? What is safe?

I do not have a good answer to the last question. I am increasingly suspicious of anyone who does.

== When no one can tell you what to become

For a long time, we acted as if preparing for the future meant predicting it. Choose the right field early. Learn the right skill. Enter the right industry. Become valuable, then remain valuable. The path was never as stable as we remember it, but it was stable enough that adults could tell a convincing story about it.

That story is harder to tell now. Technology can change faster than a degree program. A tool can move from research demo to everyday infrastructure in less time than it takes a student to finish college. A task that once required years of specialized training can become much cheaper. At the same time, entirely new forms of work can appear around tools that did not exist when the student chose a major.

The frightening conclusion is easy to reach: if the future cannot be predicted, perhaps there is no sensible way to prepare for it. But those are two different claims.

#blockquote[
An uncertain future is not the same thing as having no future.
]

Not knowing where the road goes does not mean there is no road. It means the map is incomplete. Maybe what we are losing is not a predictable future. Maybe we are losing the belief that the future was predictable.

== I have been a beginner many times

I have some personal reason to distrust the idea that a life has to be predicted before it can make sense. At eighteen, I studied audio engineering and worked in it. Then I studied electronics and worked with that. I moved deeper into computers, especially servers. I worked on games. I worked on infrastructure. I began a Ph.D. closer to computer systems. Research itself was new to me. Then ChatGPT arrived during my first semester, the landscape moved again, and over time my research moved toward HCI and computer graphics.

Written in one paragraph, that sounds almost deliberate. It was not.

I did not stand at eighteen and draw a line through audio, electronics, servers, games, infrastructure, systems, HCI, and graphics. At each point, I could usually see the next few steps. I could not see the whole path. Most of the connections became obvious only after I had already moved.

Looking backward, my life has a shape. Looking forward, it rarely did.

That distinction matters because we often read a life backward and mistake coherence for planning. Once someone has arrived somewhere, every earlier choice starts to look like preparation for the destination. The story becomes clean. Of course this led to that. Of course that skill became useful later. Of course the strange detour was secretly important.

Living forward does not feel like that. Living forward is messier. You choose with partial information. You misunderstand what you will enjoy. Industries change. Technologies change. You get good at something and discover that you want something else. Sometimes the thing you thought was the destination becomes a bridge.

A life can have continuity without having a plan.

== Starting over is not starting from nothing

One pattern in my own life is that I have repeatedly become a beginner. I have entered rooms where other people knew the vocabulary, the tools, the literature, or the craft better than I did. I have had to ask basic questions again. I have had to become visibly bad at something after becoming competent at something else.

I am only thirty-four, so I am not going to pretend that I have learned some grand lesson about aging. But I am old enough to notice that becoming a beginner feels different now than it did at eighteen.

At eighteen, being a beginner felt like: _I do not know anything yet._ Now it feels more like: _I do not know this yet._

The difference is larger than it looks.

Every time I entered a new field, I carried more with me than I first realized. Audio did not disappear when I learned electronics. Electronics did not disappear when I worked with computers. Systems thinking did not disappear when I moved toward HCI. Building things, debugging them, understanding how abstractions touch physical reality, learning new tools, working with people, recognizing when I am confused, and knowing that confusion eventually becomes less confusing: these accumulate.

The knowledge does not transfer perfectly. It should not. A new field deserves to be learned on its own terms. But becoming new at something is not the same as becoming empty.

#blockquote[
You can be a beginner in a field without being a beginner in being you.
]

There is also an emotional skill here that is easy to overlook: being willing to look unskilled again. The older we get, the more tempting it becomes to stay close to the things that already make us look competent. Expertise gives us an identity. Other people start expecting answers from us. Beginning again asks us to give up that comfort for a while.

I have not found that learning new things became impossible as I got older. What changed is that the decision to become a beginner became more expensive. There is more expertise to step away from, more opportunity cost, and perhaps more ego involved. But expensive is not impossible. And it is not always a loss.

== Preparation is not prediction

This is what I want to tell students who are trying to choose a future that none of us can clearly see: you do not need to predict the future in order to prepare for it.

Prediction asks, _What will the world reward?_ Preparation asks, _What will help me meet a world I cannot yet see?_

Those questions lead to different kinds of education.

If I am trying to predict the future, I look for the safest major, the hottest field, the skill with the longest expected half-life. I optimize for the current forecast. But if the forecast itself is unstable, this strategy becomes fragile. I may end up preparing very efficiently for a world that changed while I was preparing for it.

Preparation can be broader and deeper. Learn something hard enough to discover what deep learning feels like. Build things that fail. Stay with a difficult problem long enough to learn the difference between confusion and impossibility. Learn to work with people who do not think like you. Learn how evidence changes your mind. Learn how to become competent without needing to feel competent every day.

None of these are "AI-proof skills." I do not think we know what those are, and I am not sure such a category will remain useful.

That is almost a relief. You do not have to discover the one skill machines will never touch. You do not have to choose a profession that will remain unchanged for forty years. You do not have to turn your first expertise into your final identity.

You need enough depth to build something real, and enough looseness in your identity to learn again when reality changes.

== This is not "adapt or die"

There is a cruel version of this argument that I do not want to make.

It says that the world changes, so individuals must simply keep up. Reskill forever. Reinvent yourself. Stay relevant. If your industry disappears, move faster. If your education becomes outdated, learn at night. If AI changes your work, become more adaptable than the next person.

That turns uncertainty into a private moral test. It also lets institutions escape responsibility.

A society cannot make education expensive, careers brittle, access to new tools unequal, and technological change extremely fast, then tell young people that their only duty is to become infinitely adaptable. Universities have to change too. Employers have to invest in people, not only tools. Schools have to teach students how to use new systems without abandoning the slower work through which understanding is built. We should make it easier, not harder, for people to learn again at thirty, forty, or sixty.

A generation should not carry alone the cost of a future that its institutions also failed to predict.

So when I say that I am not afraid of becoming a beginner again, I do not mean everyone should be forced to rebuild their life every few years. Stability is valuable. Mastery is valuable. Long attention to one problem is valuable.

The freedom I want is different. It is the freedom to discover that the map changed without feeling that your life is therefore ruined.

== The future has always been hard to see

There is a line people often repeat: history does not repeat, but it rhymes. I understand why that idea is comforting. When the present feels unstable, the past gives uncertainty a shape. We look backward and find wars that ended, technologies that were absorbed, institutions that were rebuilt, and generations that entered worlds their parents could not have imagined.

But I do not think history promises that everything will be fine. Similarity is not destiny. A previous generation surviving its crisis does not guarantee that we will handle ours well. New technologies can create new kinds of concentration, new forms of inequality, and new failures that do not fit an old script.

History offers a smaller and, to me, more useful kind of hope.

People have repeatedly entered futures they could not name in advance.

Someone choosing a life before the internet could not plan around the world the internet would create. Someone choosing a career before smartphones could not list all the work that would later exist around mobile computing. Entire professions, industries, and forms of expression appeared after people had already begun becoming adults.

Their inability to predict those worlds did not mean they had no place in them.

The future has never belonged only to the people who predicted it correctly.

== Hope without a forecast

I do not want to tell an eighteen-year-old that everything will work out. Some things will not. Some jobs will disappear. Some skills will become less valuable in the market. Some institutions will respond badly. Some people will be hurt by transitions they did not choose.

Hope that requires denying those possibilities is fragile. I prefer a different definition.

#blockquote[
Hope is not certainty about the outcome. It is the belief that what we do still matters before the outcome is known.
]

That is enough reason to learn something deeply. Enough reason to make things. Enough reason to care about a problem. Enough reason to become good at something even if the exact economic value of that skill may change. Learning is not valuable only when it predicts a job correctly.

And if the world changes? Learn what changed. Carry what still transfers. Leave behind what no longer serves you. Become new at something again.

I cannot tell an eighteen-year-old today what they should become. At thirties, I am not entirely sure what I will become either. I used to think that kind of uncertainty meant I had failed to plan far enough ahead. I no longer think so.

I have been a beginner enough times to know that starting over is rarely starting from nothing.

At eighteen, I thought being a beginner meant: _I do not know anything yet._ Now I think: _I do not know this yet._

There will probably be many more things I do not know. I hope there are.

You do not need to know the future to have one. You only need to know that when the future arrives, you are still allowed to learn, to change, to care about something new, and to take part in making what comes next.
